## PCA: learn a shorter linear coordinate system

Suppose two coordinates for three clauses are `[1, 1]`, `[2, 2]`, and `[3, 3]`. Their mean is `[2, 2]`. Subtract it to get `[-1, -1]`, `[0, 0]`, and `[1, 1]`. All variation lies along the diagonal: the two original coordinates repeat the same information. Projecting onto the unit direction `[1/√2, 1/√2]` gives `[-√2, 0, √2]`. One coordinate preserves all the variation in this small example.

